      "id": "da30cd61-1f16-42a5-a2fb-4891d38429cc",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "You’re now in **Section 13.5 – Local Classes**, which completes the **nested-class story** in Java.\nI’ll explain this **from the learning perspective**, not just line-by-line, so you understand **why Java allows this** and **when you’d actually use it**.\n\n---\n\n# 1️⃣ What new concept is introduced here?\n\nYou already learned:\n\n* ✅ **Static nested classes**\n* ✅ **Inner (non-static) classes**\n\n👉 This section introduces the **third kind**:\n\n## ✅ **Local Classes**\n\n> A **local class** is a class declared **inside a method**.\n\n📌 Key rule:\n\n* It exists **only inside that method**\n* It **cannot be accessed anywhere else**\n\n---\n\n# 2️⃣ Where is the local class?\n\nInside **`Main.addPigLatinName()`** 👇\n\n```java\npublic static void addPigLatinName(List<? extends StoreEmployee> list) {\n\n    String lastName = \"Piggy\";\n\n    class DecoratedEmployee extends StoreEmployee\n            implements Comparable<DecoratedEmployee> {\n```\n\n🔹 `DecoratedEmployee` is a **local class**\n🔹 Scope = **only inside `addPigLatinName()`**\n\n---\n\n# 3️⃣ Why does Java allow local classes?\n\nBecause sometimes you need:\n\n* A **temporary helper class**\n* Used **once**\n* Closely tied to **method logic**\n\nInstead of polluting your package with a new `.java` file.\n\n💡 Think:\n\n> “I need a one-time object with behavior”\n\n---\n\n# 4️⃣ What does `DecoratedEmployee` do?\n\n### Purpose\n\nIt **wraps** an existing employee and adds:\n\n* A **Pig Latin name**\n* Sorting behavior\n\nThis is a classic **Decorator pattern**.\n\n---\n\n### Fields\n\n```java\nprivate String pigLatinName;\nprivate Employee originalInstance;\n```\n\n📌 Important:\n\n* It does **not replace** the original employee\n* It **references** it\n\n---\n\n### Constructor\n\n```java\npublic DecoratedEmployee(String pigLatinName, Employee originalInstance) {\n    this.pigLatinName = pigLatinName + \" \" + lastName;\n    this.originalInstance = originalInstance;\n}\n```\n\n⚠️ Notice:\n\n* `lastName` is from the **enclosing method**\n* It works because `lastName` is **effectively final**\n\n> Local classes can access **final or effectively-final variables**\n\n---\n\n### `toString()`\n\n```java\nreturn originalInstance.toString() + \" \" + pigLatinName;\n```\n\nCombines:\n\n* Original employee data\n* New pig-latin name\n\n---\n\n### `compareTo()`\n\n```java\nreturn pigLatinName.compareTo(o.pigLatinName);\n```\n\nSo now:\n\n* These objects can be **sorted alphabetically by pig-latin name**\n\n---\n\n# 5️⃣ How the local class is used\n\n### Step 1: Create a new list\n\n```java\nList<DecoratedEmployee> newList = new ArrayList<>(list.size());\n```\n\nWe **don’t modify** the original list.\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Convert each employee\n\n```java\nfor (var employee : list) {\n    String name = employee.getName();\n    String pigLatin = name.substring(1) + name.charAt(0) + \"ay\";\n    newList.add(new DecoratedEmployee(pigLatin, employee));\n}\n```\n\nExample:\n\n```\nMeg → egMay Piggy\nJoe → oeJay Piggy\n```\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Sort\n\n```java\nnewList.sort(null);\n```\n\n`null` means:\n\n> “Use natural ordering” → `Comparable`\n\nWhich comes from:\n\n```java\nimplements Comparable<DecoratedEmployee>\n```\n\n---\n\n### Step 4: Print\n\n```java\nSystem.out.println(dEmployee.originalInstance.getName() + \" \"\n        + dEmployee.pigLatinName);\n```\n\n---\n\n# 6️⃣ Important rules of Local Classes (EXAM / INTERVIEW)\n\n✅ Can:\n\n* Extend a class\n* Implement interfaces\n* Access **final / effectively final** local variables\n\n❌ Cannot:\n\n* Be `public`, `private`, `protected`\n* Have static members (except constants)\n* Be used outside the method\n\n---\n\n# 7️⃣ Now switch gears: `Meal`, `Item`, `Burger`\n\nThis part reinforces **inner classes** (not local classes).\n\n---\n\n## `Meal` (Outer class)\n\n```java\npublic class Meal {\n    private double price = 5.0;\n    private double conversionRate;\n}\n```\n\nRepresents a **meal order**.\n\n---\n\n## Inner class: `Item`\n\n```java\nprivate class Item {\n```\n\nWhy inner?\n\n* An `Item` **only exists inside a Meal**\n* It needs access to:\n\n  * `conversionRate`\n  * `Meal.this.price`\n\nExample:\n\n```java\nthis(name, type, type.equals(\"burger\") ? Meal.this.price : 0);\n```\n\n👉 Inner classes can access **outer class fields directly**\n\n---\n\n## Static method inside inner class\n\n```java\nprivate static double getPrice(double price, double rate)\n```\n\nAllowed because:\n\n* It doesn’t depend on instance data\n\n---\n\n## Inner class: `Burger extends Item`\n\n```java\nprivate class Burger extends Item\n```\n\nBurger is a **special Item** with:\n\n* Toppings\n* Extra pricing logic\n\n---\n\n## Nested enum inside inner class\n\n```java\nprivate enum Extra { AVOCADO, BACON, CHEESE, ... }\n```\n\nThis shows:\n\n> Java allows **deep nesting** when it makes logical sense\n\n---\n\n# 8️⃣ Why this design is excellent\n\n### ✅ Encapsulation\n\n* `Item`, `Burger`, `Extra` are hidden from the outside world\n\n### ✅ Real-world modeling\n\n* A `Burger` cannot exist without a `Meal`\n* A `Topping` cannot exist without a `Burger`\n\n### ✅ Clean API\n\nFrom `Store`:\n\n```java\nMeal meal = new Meal();\nmeal.addToppings(\"Bacon\", \"Mayo\");\nSystem.out.println(meal);\n```\n\nNo clutter, no unnecessary exposure.\n\n---\n\n# 9️⃣ Final mental model (VERY IMPORTANT)\n\n| Class Type    | Declared Where | Lifetime         | Needs Outer Instance |\n| ------------- | -------------- | ---------------- | -------------------- |\n| Top-level     | File           | App-wide         | ❌                    |\n| Static nested | Inside class   | App-wide         | ❌                    |\n| Inner         | Inside class   | Object lifetime  | ✅                    |\n| **Local**     | Inside method  | Method execution | ✅                    |\n\n---\n\n# 🔑 One-line takeaway\n\n> **Local classes are short-lived, method-scoped helper classes that exist to support one specific operation.**\n\n---\n\nIf you want next:\n\n* 🧠 Memory diagram of local vs inner classes\n* 🔁 Rewrite local class as lambda\n* ❓ When NOT to use local classes\n\nJust say 👍\n",
      "metadata": {
